1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 b6 4. g3 Bb7 5. Bg2 Be7 6. O-O O-O ({Allowing White to make the ensuing central thrust; a safer line was} 6... d5 {and if then} 7. Ne5 {,} c6) 7. d5 exd5 8. Nh4 c6 9. cxd5 Nxd5 10. Nf5 Bf6 11. Re1 Ba6 12. e4 {Fine play; he spurns the regaining of the pawn and makes for command of greater space.} ({Ingenious; [Black] allows White to win back his pawn by} 12. Bxd5 cxd5 13. Qxd5 {, but then after} Nc6 {Black’s two Bishops rake White’s position and he has the better game.}) 12... Ne7 13. Ne3 Be5 14. Ng4 Bc7 15. e5 d5 ({The one mistake that Black makes in the game — but it is enough to lose it! Correct was} 15... d6) 16. Bg5 Bc8 17. Nf6+ Kh8 (17... gxf6 18. Bxf6 Bf5 19. Qd2 {, threatening Qh6, wins for White.}) 18. Nc3 Na6 19. Nfxd5 cxd5 20. Nxd5 {White’s sacrifice was pure­ly temporary since, owing to the bishop pin on the knight, White regains his piece with interest.} Be6 21. Bxe7 Qd7 22. Nxc7 Qxc7 23. Bd6 Qc4 {This loses even more material, but he was already quite lost.} 24. Bf1 {Source: The Times, 1 May 1982 (Golombek)} (24. Bf1 {If he defends his knight by} Qc8 {, then, simply,} 25. Bxf8 Qxf8 26. Bxa6 {, etc.}) 1-0

